*Context: *Here=E2=80=99s a question I often hear on panels: Can Chinese co=
mpanies
actually resist the government, given, you know, uh, the system over there?
It=E2=80=99s a simple but essential question. And I would wager that a lot =
of
people who label themselves as =E2=80=9CChina watchers=E2=80=9D would give =
either the wrong
answer or an incoherent one. *Don=E2=80=99t worry though: they=E2=80=99ve r=
ead one article
about those party committees, so we can end the conversation there!*

To answer this question, let=E2=80=99s work through two concrete examples. =
First,
look at the pushback from businesses against China=E2=80=99s social credit =
system.
The Chinese government declared ambitions to create a comprehensive dataset
that integrates data from businesses with =E2=80=9Ccredit=E2=80=9D informat=
ion on
individuals. Yet, given the importance of proprietary datasets, why would
companies share their data with government departments (which have been lax
about data security measures in the past)? According to FT reporting,
Alibaba and Tencent *refused
<https://substack.com/redirect/207b51c3-2846-45fd-a156-7198a612f738?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
to give over customer loans data to a national credit scoring scheme. As
Martin Chorzempa and Samm Sacks *conclude
<https://substack.com/redirect/8ec93005-7c5c-4d37-af98-ec2a049bd923?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*,
=E2=80=9CThe lack of even basic data sharing is one of the most overlooked
roadblocks to constructing the social credit system.=E2=80=9D

Next, China=E2=80=99s AI regulation-making process gives us another opportu=
nity to
explore the sometimes *contentious *relationship between the Chinese
government and companies. On December 27, the Cyberspace Administration of
China released draft measures on human-like interactive AI services (*Engli=
sh
translation
<https://substack.com/redirect/2a4709c9-6054-478e-a640-3a2cc59c685c?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
from China Law Translate). The measures attempt to address the risks of
companion AI, especially for minors.

However, some of the specific planks will likely draw resistance from
companies. As noted by the excellent *Geopolitechs blog
<https://substack.com/redirect/15930278-c173-4b18-ba6f-1166d4661bd5?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
=E2=80=94 written by an analyst at a Chinese big tech company =E2=80=94 the=
 draft has
=E2=80=9Ccaused heated discussion among China=E2=80=99s AI community and dr=
awn commentary
from independent media voices.=E2=80=9D This week=E2=80=99s translation fea=
tures one of
these critical commentaries (link to *original Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/b956eff5-c4be-413b-b68d-3c2b2dbc0b89?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*)
from a WeChat public account [=E6=95=B0=E6=8D=AE=E4=BD=95=E8=A7=84] run by =
Chen He, a data compliance
manager at a large Chinese securities firm.

*Key Takeaways: Acknowledging that human-like interactive AI is a high-risk
area, Chen He writes, =E2=80=9CHowever, the Measures impose very high compl=
iance
obligations on companies, and I believe that AI companies will collectively
raise concerns.=E2=80=9D*

   -

   One likely area of pushback pertains to how AI companies obtain consent
   to exploit user data for model training. This matters. As He notes,
=E2=80=9C*High-quality
   training data is already scarce, and user interaction feedback is an
   important reference for fine-tuning and optimizing models*.=E2=80=9D Cur=
rently,
   in accordance with existing national standards (specifically, the =E2=80=
=9Cbasic
   security requirements for genAI services=E2=80=9D standard ), *companies=
 can
   have a default setting that permits utilization of user data for trainin=
g
   purposes*, with an option for users to go out of their way and disable
   it =E2=80=94 as the standard states, as long as users can =E2=80=9C*reac=
h the option
   from the main service interface in no more than 4 clicks*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Article 15 of these latest draft measures would change that. It
reads: =E2=80=9C*Except
   as otherwise provided by laws and administrative regulations, or where
   users=E2=80=99 separate consent has been obtained, providers must not us=
e users=E2=80=99
   interaction data or sensitive personal information in model training*.=
=E2=80=9D
   The key phrase here is =E2=80=9Cseparate consent,=E2=80=9D which differs=
 from a default
   setting of permissive use. To illustrate this point, He includes a
   screenshot of Doubao=E2=80=99s training data opt-out interface (pictured=
 below) and
   asks: If this permission request for your user data popped up immediatel=
y
   when you entered the app, would you allow ByteDance to mine your user da=
ta?

<https://substack.com/redirect/39ccd26f-22ad-4799-97e6-72d437afc34e?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>

*Second, if past AI regulations are any guide, the final version of these
measures will look meaningfully different. Chen He writes, =E2=80=9CIt is
anticipated that, like the =E2=80=98Interim Measures for the Management of
Generative Artificial Intelligence Services,=E2=80=99 the final version wil=
l have
significant changes compared to the draft.*

   -

   Many people have singled out the companion AI regulation=E2=80=99s provi=
sion
   that obligates AI developers to use training data that reflects =E2=80=
=9Ccore
   socialist values=E2=80=9D (Article 6). I would predict that this gets wa=
tered down
   in later versions, as this degree of scrutiny on training data will be v=
ery
   difficult to enforce and implement.
   -

   Again, we can trace this back-and-forth dispute through the development
   of past regulations on generative AI services. In the initial draft,
   released in April 2023, the Chinese government mandated that AI develope=
rs
   ensure the =E2=80=9C*veracity, accuracy, objectivity, and diversity*=E2=
=80=9D of their
   training data. In the final draft, the measures were softened considerab=
ly,
   as AI developers only needed to =E2=80=9C*employ effective measures
   <https://substack.com/redirect/3e45a461-86a0-4eb8-8c7b-918d87bbefc1?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
   to increase the quality of training data, and increase the truth, accura=
cy,
   objectivity, and diversity of training data*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   The aforementioned basic security requirements standard also went
   through a similar process, as documented in *ChinAI #271: Key Chinese
   GenAI Security Standard Changelog
   <https://substack.com/redirect/e970e6c8-ceab-47c8-85a5-9c1bcd172aff?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>.
   *For example, the initial version demanded that a=E2=80=9Cblacklist of c=
orpus
   sources should be established, and data from blacklisted sources should =
not
   be used for training.=E2=80=9D Yet, in later drafts, this blacklist
requirement was *dropped
   <https://substack.com/redirect/88b5c050-b6a3-4cde-a6cd-cbb00a8e39db?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   .

To conclude, permit me a brief meditation on the opening question. Why is
it so hard for us to even conceive of the possibility that Chinese firms
pursue interests that diverge from the government? I don=E2=80=99t have the=
 answers
here. And, of course, contentious business-state relations will look
different in an authoritarian context. I do know this: If you=E2=80=99re a =
data
compliance manager or a policy strategist at a Chinese company, the answer
is obvious =E2=80=94 pushing back against the government is just part of th=
e job.
Are we reading them?

