latory Pathways =E2=80=94 Big Tech Firms are Sleepless</a></strong></h2><p =
style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:1=
6px"><strong>Context: </strong><span>Here=E2=80=99s a question I often hear=
 on panels: Can Chinese companies actually resist the government, given, yo=
u know, uh, the system over there? It=E2=80=99s a simple but essential ques=
tion. And I would wager that a lot of people who label themselves as =E2=80=
=9CChina watchers=E2=80=9D would give either the wrong answer or an incoher=
ent one. </span><em>Don=E2=80=99t worry though: they=E2=80=99ve read one ar=
ticle about those party committees, so we can end the conversation there!</=
em></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;f=
ont-size:16px"><span>To answer this question, let=E2=80=99s work through tw=
o concrete examples. First, look at the pushback from businesses against Ch=
ina=E2=80=99s social credit system. The Chinese government declared ambitio=
ns to create a comprehensive dataset that integrates data from businesses w=
ith =E2=80=9Ccredit=E2=80=9D information on individuals. Yet, given the imp=
ortance of proprietary datasets, why would companies share their data with =
government departments (which have been lax about data security measures in=
 the past)? According to FT reporting, Alibaba and Tencent </span><strong><=
a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/207b51c3-2846-45fd-a156-7198a612f73=
8?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=
=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_bl=
ank">refused</a></strong><span> to give over customer loans data to a natio=
nal credit scoring scheme. As Martin Chorzempa and Samm Sacks </span><stron=
g><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/8ec93005-7c5c-4d37-af98-ec2a049b=
d923?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" re=
l=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_b=
lank">conclude</a></strong><span>, =E2=80=9CThe lack of even basic data sha=
ring is one of the most overlooked roadblocks to constructing the social cr=
edit system.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,=
55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><span>Next, China=E2=80=99s AI regu=
lation-making process gives us another opportunity to explore the sometimes=
 </span><em><strong>contentious </strong></em><span>relationship between th=
e Chinese government and companies. On December 27, the Cyberspace Administ=
ration of China released draft measures on human-like interactive AI servic=
